/**
@page vector_dispatch Vector dispatch and the capability ladder

@tableofcontents

LAME's hottest quantization loops carry hand-written SIMD implementations that
are chosen at run time from the instruction sets the executing CPU reports. This
page records the shape of that dispatch ladder and, in particular, the three
decisions that are easy to second-guess later: why the ladder has no SSE4.1 or
SSE4.2 rung, why the variable-bitrate noise routine is SSE2-only with no AVX2
tier, and why it stops at AVX2 rather than carrying an AVX-512 tier above it.
All three are counter-intuitive, so the reasoning is kept here rather than only
in the commit history &mdash; the last of them because the code was written and
the answer still turned out to be no, which is exactly the kind of result that
gets repeated if it is not written down.

@section vector_dispatch_ladder The ladder

@c vector_implementation() returns a @c vector_impl_t naming the highest tier the
CPU offers: @c VECTOR_IMPL_NONE (scalar), @c VECTOR_IMPL_SSE2 or
@c VECTOR_IMPL_AVX2. Every dispatch site tests <em>">= the tier my routine
needs"</em>, so a routine that exists only at a lower tier is still selected on a
machine that offers a higher one. There is exactly one selector and one
enumerator; adding a tier is one branch in the selector and one name in the enum.

The ladder is deliberately <b>scalar &rarr; SSE2 &rarr; AVX2</b>, with nothing
between SSE2 and AVX2. On x86-64 the SSE2 tier needs no feature probe at all:
every x86-64 CPU has SSE2, as does every 32-bit x86 CPU since 2001, so the SSE2
win is unconditional. Only the AVX2 tier is gated on a run-time feature bit. The
front-end exposes two levers: @c --noasm @c avx2 disables just the AVX2 tier and
keeps SSE2, and @c --noasm @c sse turns the vector code off entirely.

@section vector_dispatch_no_sse4 Why there is no SSE4.1 / SSE4.2 tier

For every routine vectorized here, an SSE4.1 or SSE4.2 implementation is no
faster than &mdash; and usually slower than &mdash; the SSE2 one. This inverts
the usual "a newer instruction set is faster" intuition, and the reason is
specific and measurable.

The largest win is the Huffman table search (@c ix_max(), @c choose_table()):
find a region's maximum quantized coefficient and sum code lengths over table
lookups. Every value it touches is bounded by @c IXMAX_VAL @c = @c 8206, which is
below 2<sup>15</sup> = 32768, so all of it fits in signed 16-bit lanes. That one
fact inverts the instruction-set ranking:

- <b>SSE2</b> has the packed 16-bit integer operations this needs &mdash;
  @c pmaxsw (max), @c pminsw (clamp), @c packssdw (pack two 32-bit vectors into
  one 16-bit vector) and @c pmaddwd (the lookup's index arithmetic in one
  instruction) &mdash; and processes <b>eight lanes per instruction</b>.
- <b>SSE4.1</b>'s relevant additions (@c pmaxsd, @c pminsd, @c pmulld,
  @c pextrd) work on 32-bit lanes: <b>four lanes per instruction</b>. For data
  that fits in 16 bits, that is half the width for no benefit.

Measured on a Core i7-10700 (GCC 16.1, ns per element at region length 576):
the region maximum is about a quarter faster in the 8-lane SSE2 form than in
SSE4.1's @c pmaxsd (0.058 vs 0.082 ns), and the table lookup's manual gather is
roughly 3.0&times; scalar under SSE2 versus only about 1.8&times; under SSE4.1.
SSE4.1 loses on both primitives.

SSE4.2 adds only string and CRC instructions (@c pcmpestri, @c pcmpistri,
@c crc32) over SSE4.1 &mdash; none of them a numeric SIMD operation an audio
encoder's inner loops can use &mdash; so for this work SSE4.2 is identical to
SSE4.1, i.e. "nothing over SSE2". This is not academic: the project's FreeBSD
validation host is a Westmere Xeon (SSE4.2, no AVX at all), and it receives the
entire table-search win because that win lives at the SSE2 baseline and needs
nothing above it.

The float routines (@c quantize_lines_xrpow(), @c calc_sfb_noise_x34()) do not
need SSE4.x either: SSE2 already has the full single-precision float instruction
set (@c mulps, @c addps, @c subps, @c cvttps2dq), and SSE4.1's float additions
(@c dpps, @c blendps, @c roundps, @c insertps) buy nothing here &mdash; there is
no dot product, no blend, and the truncation is @c cvttps (SSE2), not a
rounding-mode @c roundps.

@subsection vector_dispatch_saturation The 16-bit correctness constraint

@c packssdw saturates at 32767, so the 16-bit maximum is a drop-in for
@c ix_max() only <em>as @c choose_table() uses it</em>: the result is only ever
tested against @c IXMAX_VAL and a @c linbits bucket, and @c IXMAX_VAL @c = @c 8206
is far below the saturation point. Values that matter are therefore exact, and
anything larger clamps to 32767, which still lands in the "> @c IXMAX_VAL, give
up" branch. A compile-time assertion beside @c ix_max_sse2() (in @c takehiro.c)
fails the build if @c IXMAX_VAL is ever raised past the saturation point. The
trick is correct for this one caller, not in general.

@section vector_dispatch_no_vbr_avx2 Why calc_sfb_noise_x34() has no AVX2 tier

@c calc_sfb_noise_x34() estimates per-scalefactor-band quantization noise; it is
a variable-bitrate routine (the constant-bitrate path never calls it). An AVX2
version was written, measured, and dropped: on a Core i7-10700 it ran at
1.00&times; &plusmn; 0.01 over the SSE2 tier for a whole VBR encode on both GCC
and MSVC &mdash; inside the measurement noise, against a real 6&ndash;8% the same
AVX2 tier delivers on the constant-bitrate routines. Three reasons compound:

1. <b>Gather-bound.</b> Each four-element block does two data-dependent table
   lookups (@c adj43[] in the quantizer step, @c pow43[] in the noise step). AVX2
   would do them as one eight-wide @c vgatherdps, but on Skylake-family and older
   cores @c vgatherdps is microcoded &mdash; roughly one element loaded per cycle
   internally, i.e. the same scalar loads wearing one opcode. It removes the
   reassembly shuffles, which were never the cost.

2. <b>The reduction is pinned to a four-block.</b> To keep every CPU of an
   architecture bit-identical, the sum of squares is reduced as
   (x0&sup2;+x2&sup2;)+(x1&sup2;+x3&sup2;) per four elements into a running
   scalar. An AVX2 tier processing eight at a time must still reduce its two
   halves separately, in that order &mdash; it cannot carry an eight-wide
   accumulator without reassociating the sum and moving the output per-CPU. So
   width helps only the cheap multiply / subtract / square around the two
   expensive gathers.

3. <b>Amdahl, twice.</b> The SSE2 vectorization of this routine already moves a
   whole VBR encode by only about 6% on MSVC and about 0% on GCC (whose compiler
   auto-vectorizes the scalar loop competently), because VBR spends most of its
   time in the psychoacoustic model, not here. AVX2's increment is a fraction of
   that already-small share.

The capability ladder explicitly permits a routine to live at one tier only.
@c calc_sfb_noise_x34() is the converse of an AVX2-only routine: an SSE2 routine
with no AVX2 version, because AVX2 has nothing to offer it. Carrying a second
tier that cannot be shown to do anything is pure cost &mdash; a translation unit,
a build entry, a dispatch branch and a test path &mdash; so it is not carried.

@section vector_dispatch_no_avx512 Why there is no AVX-512 tier

An AVX-512 tier above AVX2 was written, taken to hardware that has the
instruction set, measured end to end on three machines from two vendors, and
dropped. The SSE4.x decision above rests on microbenchmarks and an
instruction-count argument; this one rests on timed encodes of the finished
code, and the timing said no.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_built What was built

Two stages, separate because they carried different risks:

- <b>Stage 1</b> &mdash; the run-time rung, plus AVX-512 forms of the Huffman
  table search (@c ix_max(), @c choose_table()) and the quantization loop
  (@c quantize_lines_xrpow()). These are elementwise, or reduce with an
  order-independent maximum, so widening them cannot move the output. This is
  the stage where the extra width was free of any correctness tax.
- <b>Stage 2</b> &mdash; @c calc_sfb_noise_x34(), in two reduction orders: one
  reproducing the narrower tier's pinned per-four-block association exactly, and
  an opt-in freely-reducing form built only to price what that pinning costs.

The tier was confined to alpha builds and disabled itself once the version type
moved on, so it could not reach a release while the question was open.

No machine in the project has AVX-512 and QEMU's TCG does not emulate it, so
none of it could be executed here. It was compiled by four toolchains,
disassembled and read instruction by instruction, and covered by unit tests
&mdash; and then sent to lame-dev as a test kit, because what was left genuinely
needed the hardware.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_tested What was tested, and where

Three machines ran the kit, on Linux, encoding 16-bit stereo 44.1&nbsp;kHz
music &mdash; between them the first execution of these instructions anywhere:

| CPU | microarchitecture | compiler | what it ran |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| AMD Ryzen 9 7950X3D | Zen 4 | GCC 13.3 | both stages, tests and timings |
| Intel Core i5-1035G1 | Ice Lake, client | GCC 14.2 | both stages, tests and timings |
| AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D | Zen 5 | GCC 15 | stage 1, timings only |

- <b>Unit tests, stage 1: pass</b> on both machines that ran them. The kernels
  agree with the scalar reference across every region length, threshold and
  clamp boundary, index extreme and odd-length tail.
- <b>Encoder output, stage 1: bit-identical</b> on both machines that ran it, at
  @c -V2 and at @c -b @c 320. A full encode with the tier active matched the
  same build with @c --noasm @c avx512, which is the check that covers the seams
  unit tests do not &mdash; notably @c count_bit_ESC()'s clamped-count
  accounting. Stage 1 is correct.
- <b>Unit tests, stage 2: the same single failure</b> on both, across two
  vendors and two compilers. @c calc_sfb_noise_x34() does not reproduce the
  narrower tier's result exactly. This is the routine whose sum chooses a
  scalefactor, so its test asserts bit-exactness against that tier rather than a
  tolerance, and the reduction is where the two part company: the pinned
  per-four-block association did not survive widening to sixteen lanes. A tier
  that cannot reproduce the existing output is not a candidate, independently of
  its speed.

Stage 2's <em>encoder</em> output was bit-identical anyway, on both machines.
That belongs here as a caution rather than as reassurance. The unit test drives
the routine across inputs an encode does not happen to produce, so it is the
more sensitive instrument; identical output on one clip is not evidence that two
tiers agree, and the exact-match test is the one to believe. Its companion test
&mdash; the one that asserts the harness <em>can</em> report a disagreement
&mdash; passed on both machines, so the failure is the code and not the
scaffolding.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_speed The measurement

A whole encode after a warm-up, with @c --noasm @c avx512 selecting the AVX2
tier as the comparison. Stage 1 only: it is the bit-identical stage, so it is
the only one whose speed could ever have mattered.

| CPU | @c -V2 | @c -b @c 320 | runs each way |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |
| Zen 4 7950X3D | wash | <b>1.0% slower</b> | 3 |
| Zen 5 9950X3D | wash | <b>no difference</b> | 25 |
| Ice Lake i5-1035G1 | wash | <b>7.6% faster</b> | 3 |

@c -V2 is a wash on all three, inside run-to-run noise every time. Constant
bitrate at 320&nbsp;kbit/s is where the quantization loop does the most work per
frame, so it is the case with the most for stage 1 to win &mdash; and it is the
case where the three machines disagree. The disagreement is the result.

The two AMD numbers are the solid ones. On Zen 4 the arms do not overlap: three
AVX2 runs spanning 24&nbsp;ms, three AVX-512 runs spanning 8&nbsp;ms, every
AVX-512 run slower than every AVX2 run &mdash; and the same sign and magnitude
came back from a second run on a different input file, so it is not a property
of the clip. On Zen 5 the measurement was taken under a benchmarking harness
over 25 runs each way and returned 1.655&nbsp;s against 1.655&nbsp;s at
&sigma;&nbsp;0.005&nbsp;s. Not close; equal.

The Intel number is the one that could reopen the question, and its method
cannot yet carry that weight. Three sequential runs per arm on a 15&nbsp;W
mobile part, AVX-512 always first, so thermal drift across the measurement is
aligned with the arm being measured. That machine's own data bounds the drift:
its AVX2 arm ran identical code in the stage-1 and stage-2 builds and differed
by 1.6% between them &mdash; five times the spread the desktop part showed under
a harness. Pushing the other way, Ice Lake reduces clock frequency for 512-bit
work, so a genuine win there would be a win against a headwind. It is worth
re-measuring properly rather than either believing or dismissing.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_why Why the width buys nothing

The obvious explanation is the datapath: Zen 4 executes 512-bit operations as
two 256-bit halves, so a sixteen-wide instruction does no more per cycle than
the eight-wide one it replaces. That is true of Zen 4, and it is not the reason.
Zen 5 is not narrower &mdash; its desktop parts are reported to have widened
that path &mdash; and Zen 5 measured at zero, not at some fraction of a win. If
width were the binding constraint, relieving it would have shown something.

What remains is a property of the loops rather than of any CPU, which is why it
is expected to hold on Intel too:

1. <b>The same two constraints as the AVX2 analysis above, unchanged.</b> The
   table-search kernels are bounded by @c IXMAX_VAL and already run eight 16-bit
   lanes at the SSE2 baseline; the band-noise kernel is gather-bound and its
   reduction is pinned to the narrower tier's association. Doubling the register
   width relieves none of that. Stage 2 is the demonstration: unpinning the
   reduction is what widening actually requires, and doing so cost the tier its
   correctness.

2. <b>Amdahl.</b> What was left to win after AVX2 is a small share of the encode,
   and a fraction of a small share is not measurable against run-to-run spread.

Zen 4's halved datapath is then a third-order detail. It explains why Zen 4
landed slightly on the wrong side of zero rather than exactly on it.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_open What is still open

One controlled measurement on Intel hardware.

The Ice Lake report is the only Intel number there is, and the caveats under the
measurement above are the reason it does not settle anything by itself. What
would settle it is the protocol the Zen 5 report used: many runs each way under
a harness that reports a spread, on a part that is not thermally constrained, on
16-bit stereo 44.1&nbsp;kHz music. If a gain of that size reproduces, the ladder
grows a rung and this section is wrong. If it collapses towards zero, the
question is closed.

The reasoning predicts zero. Intel's full-width parts remove the datapath
argument &mdash; but the Zen 5 result has already shown that argument was not
the binding one, and what is left is the two loop-level constraints above.

Two notes on scope. Intel's client parts have carried AVX-512 only within a
window &mdash; Ice Lake through Tiger Lake and Rocket Lake, withdrawn from Alder
Lake onwards, and not returning until AVX10 &mdash; so the machine that produced
the outlying number is from that window rather than representative of current
client hardware, and Zen 4 and Zen 5 remain most of the population such a tier
would reach. And the stage-2 failure is not a performance question at all: it
would have to be resolved before any Intel number could put the band-noise
routine back on the table, however fast it ran.

@section vector_dispatch_consequence Consequence

There is no @c VECTOR_IMPL_SSE41 / @c VECTOR_IMPL_SSE42 enumerator, no configure
probe and no dispatch branch for them. Every x86-64 CPU, and every 32-bit x86 CPU
since 2001, receives the full table-search and quantization win at the SSE2
baseline the build already guarantees; AVX2 adds a further 6&ndash;8% on constant
bitrate and nothing measurable on variable bitrate.

There is no @c VECTOR_IMPL_AVX512 enumerator either, for a different reason: not
that the case was never made, but that it was built, taken to three machines and
measured, and came back at zero on the two that could measure it precisely.
AVX2 is the top of the ladder, with one controlled Intel measurement outstanding
against that.
*/
